#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Spend the palette.

    legion-theme          write the palette out of theme/palette.toml
    legion-theme --check  say what it would change, change nothing

theme/palette.toml is the one place a colour is decided. Everything on the
machine reads from there, and almost nothing on the machine holds a hex.

Almost. A stylesheet, a Lua table, a TOML file, an ini file, a shell script
and a browser cannot share a variable with each other, but most of them can
import a file written in their own language. So this writes one small palette
file per language, and the stylesheet, the terminal, the keyboard and the
browser each import the one that speaks theirs. Change a colour and they all
change, because none of them holds a copy.

Two cannot import anything. KDE's ini format has no include and Firefox's
user.js is a list of literals, so those two get the colours written into them
between a pair of markers. The pictures are drawn.

Nothing here is installed. This writes into files/ and `legion apply` puts
those on the machine, so the palette goes through the same manifest as
everything else and `legion check` reports a drifted colour like any other
drift.
"""

import argparse
import math
import struct
import sys
import tomllib
import zlib
